WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.460
If you can match every object with exactly one partner

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:06.620
of the opposite type, then exactly half the objects are

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:07.350
of each type.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.250
A good pairing lets you count without listing anything.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.630
Pairing arguments are short and they are proofs.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.220
They explain why the answer is half, instead of just

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.910
noticing it in a few examples.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.440
Three light switches can each be on or off,

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:23.580
giving eight settings.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.740
In how many of them is an even number of switches on?

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.480
Zero counts as even.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:30.680
Pause and try to predict the answer.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.920
Pair each setting with the one you get by flipping

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:37.890
the first switch.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.280
Flip it again and you return, so the settings really

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.190
are in pairs.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.320
No setting is its own partner,

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.830
because flipping always changes something.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.720
Flipping one switch changes the number of switches that

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.230
are on by exactly one.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.360
So in each pair, one setting has an even number on and

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.810
the other has an odd number.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.610
Four pairs, so four settings are even and four are odd.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.230
The same argument works whenever there is at least one

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:06.140
switch to flip.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.850
With ten switches there are one thousand and twenty-four

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.850
settings, and exactly half, five hundred and twelve,

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.880
have an even number on.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.120
No listing needed.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.090
Pairing also adds.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.350
To add the numbers from one to one hundred,

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.800
pair one with one hundred, two with ninety-nine,

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:22.940
and so on.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.735
There are fifty pairs, each adding to one

00:01:25.735 --> 00:01:26.420
hundred and one.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:28.980
The total is five thousand and fifty.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.400
Always check that nothing is left without a partner,

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:33.720
or paired with itself.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.330
The factors of thirty-six pair up to multiply to

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.400
thirty-six, except six, which pairs with itself.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.620
That is why thirty-six has an odd number of factors.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.910
Find a rule that pairs every object with

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:47.800
a different partner.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:49.940
If each pair has one of each type,

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.640
the types occur equally often.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.210
Notebook challenge: what is the sum of the whole numbers

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:56.560
from one to forty?
